+## 18. Addendum: Wayland as the primary Linux target
+
+### 18.1 Architectural effect
+
+Retain C++20, Qt Widgets, the flow interfaces, pairing model, SQLite and grouped operations. Cullfinch is a Wayland **client**. Qt supplies the platform integration; there is no need for application-specific Wayland protocol code or a compositor dependency. The versus panes and wall tiles are items/widgets inside one top-level comparison window, so their arrangement remains fully controlled by Cullfinch. [Qt's Wayland client support](https://doc.qt.io/qt-6/wayland-and-qt.html)
+
+Build and deploy the Qt Wayland client integration plugin. Qt Wayland Compositor is a separate facility for building display servers and is not an application dependency. The client plugin is available under free-software licences, including LGPLv3; do not infer the required client licence from the compositor module's licence. [Qt Wayland licensing and module roles](https://doc.qt.io/qt-6/qtwaylandcompositor-index.html)
+
+Prefer native Wayland when launching in a supported Wayland desktop session, while respecting an explicit user-selected Qt backend. Keep `-platform xcb` as a documented compatibility option. A Wayland failure must be diagnosable rather than hidden by an automatic XWayland fallback. Record Qt version, platform-plugin name and relevant rendering information in diagnostics.
+
+Use a normal decorated main window and Qt's existing menu/dialog APIs. Custom borderless chrome is unnecessary for the initial requirements and would add compositor-specific move/resize work. Application shortcuts are active within Cullfinch; the design needs no global hotkeys, desktop-wide pointer control, or access to other applications' windows.
+
+### 18.2 Window state, fullscreen and input
+
+Use Qt's fullscreen/window-state APIs and respond to the size actually configured by the compositor. Size the wall from the comparison widget's current content rectangle, not a saved screen geometry. Fullscreen transitions are asynchronous; wait for relevant state/resize/exposure events and preserve the comparison state throughout.
+
+Treat a requested output as a preference subject to compositor behaviour. Default fullscreen to the current window's output. Preserve normal window size, maximised/fullscreen intent and internal view settings; do not promise restoration to an exact global desktop coordinate. Qt documents that `QWindow::setPosition()` is unsupported on Wayland. [QWindow system movement](https://doc.qt.io/qt-6/qwindow.html#startSystemMove)
+
+Avoid focus-stealing assumptions. Returning from a file dialog, switching workspaces, Alt-Tab or unlocking the desktop must preserve decisions and restore internal widget focus when the application becomes active again. A focus loss, output removal or intervening resize cancels an armed rejection gesture. Opening or closing a window is never itself a culling decision.
+
+Hit-test using widget/scene-local coordinates, asset IDs and layout revisions. Do not depend on global cursor coordinates, cursor warping or X11 window identifiers. Popups/dialogs have the correct parent and modality, and remain usable in fullscreen. Existing reflow/double-click protections apply equally at every scale factor.
+
+### 18.3 Scaling and 100% inspection
+
+Define three distinct units: source-image pixels, Qt logical coordinates, and rendered buffer pixels. Layout and pointer events use logical coordinates. Decode/cache sizing uses the target physical buffer size. Qt separates logical widget geometry from raw image-buffer pixels, and further compositor scaling can occur after rendering. [Qt high-DPI model](https://doc.qt.io/qt-6/highdpi.html)
+
+Use the target window's device-pixel ratio, not only the screen's ratio. Qt explicitly notes that those values can differ on Wayland with fractional scaling. Recalculate preview size and cache requests when the window changes output, scale or size; discard obsolete asynchronous results by request revision. [QScreen device-pixel-ratio guidance](https://doc.qt.io/qt-6/qscreen.html#devicePixelRatio-prop)
+
+For Cullfinch, **100% inspection means one source-image pixel per rendered buffer pixel**. The corresponding logical extent is source extent divided by the effective window device-pixel ratio. Account for any QImage/QPixmap ratio already applied so scaling is not applied twice. Keep linked pan/zoom in source or normalised image coordinates across windows/outputs.
+
+Do not claim a guaranteed one-to-one mapping to physical panel pixels on every fractional-scaling configuration. A compositor may resample the surface. Make fit-to-view versus 100% explicit, use full-resolution decoded pixels for inspection, and verify actual sharpness on representative desktops. No hidden sharpening or thumbnail upscaling may stand in for a ready full-resolution view.
+
+Acceptance cases include 100%, 125%, 150% and 200% desktop scaling where supported; mixed-scale monitors; an output disappearing while fullscreen; and a scale/size change between pointer press and release. Synthetic Qt scale overrides help test geometry but do not substitute for a real compositor's fractional-scale path.
+
+### 18.4 File dialogs, desktop identity and distribution
+
+Keep folder selection behind the existing desktop/UI boundary and use QFileDialog. Include the Qt platform-theme/desktop-integration support needed by the selected package and test native or portal-backed folder selection on the supported desktops. The freedesktop file-chooser portal supports selecting a directory; use existing Qt integration instead of implementing its protocol in Cullfinch. [QFileDialog](https://doc.qt.io/qt-6/qfiledialog.html), [file-chooser portal](https://flatpak.github.io/xdg-desktop-portal/docs/doc-org.freedesktop.portal.FileChooser.html)
+
+A Wayland desktop does not by itself make an ordinary AppImage sandboxed. Keep the existing local filesystem, RAW association and Trash design. If Flatpak packaging is later added, separately verify selected-directory access, durable access on reopen, discovery of companion files and creation/restoration of the staging directory. Access to one selected JPG alone would not satisfy the grouped-file requirement.
+
+Set a stable desktop application ID, match the installed `.desktop` basename, and provide corresponding icon metadata. Use `QGuiApplication::setDesktopFileName()` consistently so the desktop can identify Cullfinch for grouping and presentation. Choose the namespace after the repository owner is known; the product/executable names are already fixed. [Qt desktop-file identity](https://doc.qt.io/qt-6/qguiapplication.html#desktopFileName-prop)
+
+The installed package must contain the correct Qt Wayland plugin and its required integration dependencies. In package smoke tests, clear build-machine Qt search paths and run with native Wayland explicitly selected and X11 unavailable. A package that works only through the XCB plugin does not satisfy the primary Linux target. Test desktop decorations, folder selection and app-icon grouping on both KDE Plasma and GNOME.
+
+Retain the initial sRGB-oriented image policy and the stated colour-management limitations. Wayland targeting alone does not establish calibrated display output or HDR support. Verify the pinned Qt rendering path and the compositor's colour behaviour before expanding that promise; avoid double-applying output transforms.
+
+### 18.5 Native Wayland in GitHub Actions
+
+Promote the complete core GUI suite and installed-package smoke test to **required PR checks under native Wayland**. Use Weston as the deterministic reference compositor in CI; its documented headless backend is intended for testing. Select and pin a supported Weston release and its software-rendering configuration during the first CI spike. [Weston backends](https://wayland.pages.freedesktop.org/weston/toc/running-weston.html)
+
+`tests/support/with-wayland.sh` owns a bounded, isolated session:
+
+1. Create a dedicated runtime directory owned by the runner user with mode 0700, a unique Wayland socket name and a separate test application-data/cache root.
+2. Start a private D-Bus session when desktop integration needs it. Start Weston with the headless backend, a pinned desktop-shell configuration and an explicit test output size; use a supported software renderer so the baseline job does not require a GPU.
+3. Wait for an actual successful Wayland client connection with a deadline, checking compositor liveness; the socket's existence alone is insufficient readiness evidence.
+4. Export `WAYLAND_DISPLAY` and `QT_QPA_PLATFORM=wayland` for the tests, and unset `DISPLAY` in that test process environment. Assert that Qt reports the expected native Wayland backend; reject XCB, offscreen or minimal backends in this suite.
+5. Run CTest with bounded test timeouts, collect app/compositor logs and capture Cullfinch's own widget renderings on failure.
+6. Terminate the owned compositor/session processes, reap them and clean temporary state through exit traps, preserving failure artifacts.
+
+Use QTest's in-process input for the core widget scenarios, after the real Wayland window has been configured and exposed. This tests Cullfinch's behaviour and its native presentation path, but does not prove the compositor's real keyboard/pointer routing. A headless compositor can lack a physical input seat, so do not make every test depend on successful desktop activation or synthetic global input.
+
+Keep a distinct compositor/input integration suite for real pointer/keyboard focus, native dialogs, decorations and workspace transitions. Run supported automated cases in controlled KDE/GNOME sessions and record any manual release cases explicitly. Do not use X11-only automation such as xdotool to claim native Wayland coverage, or substitute an XWayland process for a failing native test.
+
+Take visual-regression images from Cullfinch's own widget rendering, and label their scope accordingly. They do not capture final desktop composition, window decorations or physical output colour/scaling. Those behaviours need compositor/output smoke checks. Desktop screenshot permission or global screen capture is not required for the core application test suite.
+
+Run the canonical GCC coverage job through the same native Wayland session helper. Retain the single combined Codecov report for unit, integration and GUI tests. X11 smoke remains a secondary compatibility check; macOS Cocoa testing remains unchanged.
+
+### 18.6 Additional release acceptance
+
+| Area | Required evidence for the Wayland-primary baseline |
+|---|---|
+| Backend | Installed Cullfinch starts natively without XWayland/X11 or build-tree plugins |
+| Comparison | Versus and wall, selection, inspection, undo and safe rejection gestures pass under native Wayland |
+| Window state | Fullscreen enter/exit, maximised/normal transitions and internal focus work without absolute-position assumptions |
+| Scaling | Correct render resolution, hit-testing and 100% semantics across supported integer/fractional scales |
+| Multiple outputs | Moving between outputs and disconnecting an output preserve decisions and recover a usable view |
+| Desktop integration | Folder chooser cancellation/success, dialog parenting, decorations and app identity work on KDE Plasma and GNOME |
+| Colour/rendering | No unintended image transforms or thumbnail substitution; documented limitations remain visible in release notes |
+| CI | Native Wayland is a required PR/backend gate and the coverage path; logs identify the compositor and Qt backend |
+
+The meaningful change from revision 0.2 is that native Wayland becomes the main Linux validation path. The photo model, comparison engines and grouped operations keep their existing contracts.
